WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.700
Imagine sending positively charged alpha particles toward an extremely thin

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.280
metal foil.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.260
Most pass through with little change in direction.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:14.680
A small fraction turn sharply, and a very few come back toward the source.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.540
Those rare events are the important clue.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.960
A diffuse positive charge spread throughout an atom cannot easily produce such

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.400
strong deflections in this picture.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.520
Rutherford's nuclear model puts the positive charge and almost all the mass

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.480
into a tiny central nucleus.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.520
Most of the atomic volume lies outside that nucleus.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.480
Please do not interpret the phrase mostly empty space as saying that quantum

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.960
electron distributions have no physical role.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.720
It is a comparison of the very small nuclear size with the much larger atomic

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:48.120
size.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.325
An alpha particle has charge plus two e, and a nucleus with atomic number Z

00:00:54.325 --> 00:00:54.540
has

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.580
charge plus Z e.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.180
Their repulsion can bend the alpha particle's path.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.300
A particle passing far away feels a smaller deflecting influence than one

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.140
approaching closely.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.320
Think for a moment: which observation supports a small concentrated nucleus

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.040
most directly?

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.420
It is the rare large angle scattering, especially backward scattering, not

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.620
simply the fact that many particles pass through.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.340
The experiment changed the structure we assign to an atom.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.200
But a classical planetary model still faces a problem: an accelerating

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:27.680
orbiting

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.500
charge should radiate energy.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.720
Bohr's model will introduce new postulates to address atomic stability and

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.520
discrete spectra.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.296
Let's work out one clean scattering case rather than guessing from the

00:01:39.296 --> 00:01:39.700
picture.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.440
An alpha particle approaches a heavy nucleus directly along the line joining

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.060
their centers.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.300
Treat the nucleus as fixed, neglecting recoil.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.060
At the closest point of this head on approach, the alpha particle momentarily

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.780
stops before moving back.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.460
Its initial kinetic energy has become electric potential energy of repulsion.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:05.355
Taking potential energy zero at infinite separation, write K equals k times

00:02:05.355 --> 00:02:05.560
two

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.660
e times Z e divided by r minimum.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.320
Rearranging gives r minimum equal to two Z k e squared divided by K.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.440
The factor two comes from the alpha particle's charge.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.620
A useful constant is k e squared approximately one point four four mega

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.300
electron volts times femtometers.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.000
For a gold nucleus, Z is seventy nine.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.580
Let the incident alpha energy be seven point two mega electron volts.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.680
The distance is two times seventy nine times one point four four divided by

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.700
seven point two, giving thirty one point six femtometers.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.900
That is a center to center distance, not the alpha particle's traveled

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:44.380
distance.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.380
It is outside the approximate gold nuclear radius, so the Coulomb model is

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.540
sensible for this illustration.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.860
Your turn: if incident energy doubles, what happens to this head on closest

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.220
distance?

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.500
It halves.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.960
For a non head on trajectory, motion has an angular component at closest

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.260
approach, so you cannot say all kinetic energy has vanished.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.800
Use the head on formula only when that condition is stated.

00:03:09.168 --> 00:03:13.245
Bohr kept a nuclear center but added rules that classical motion alone does

00:03:13.245 --> 00:03:13.408
not

00:03:13.408 --> 00:03:13.708
supply.

00:03:14.608 --> 00:03:17.928
Certain stationary states are allowed, and an electron in one of those states

00:03:17.928 --> 00:03:20.268
does not continuously radiate energy in the model.

00:03:21.068 --> 00:03:26.328
The angular momentum is restricted to n times h divided by two pi, with n a

00:03:26.328 --> 00:03:26.968
positive integer.

00:03:28.128 --> 00:03:31.228
Combining that postulate with Coulomb force providing the centripetal force

00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:33.148
gives allowed radii and energies.

00:03:34.328 --> 00:03:38.277
For a hydrogen like system containing exactly one electron, the energy is

00:03:38.277 --> 00:03:38.548
minus

00:03:38.548 --> 00:03:42.888
thirteen point six times Z squared divided by n squared, in electron volts.

00:03:44.148 --> 00:03:48.568
The radius is zero point zero five two nine times n squared divided by Z, in

00:03:48.568 --> 00:03:48.968
nanometers.

00:03:49.768 --> 00:03:52.708
We neglect reduced mass corrections in these introductory formulas.

00:03:53.948 --> 00:03:57.708
Negative energy means the electron is bound, with the zero chosen for a

00:03:57.708 --> 00:03:58.648
separated electron and nucleus.

00:03:59.648 --> 00:04:01.368
It does not mean negative kinetic energy.

00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:04.008
For hydrogen, Z is one.

00:04:04.628 --> 00:04:08.808
At n equal to two, energy is minus three point four electron volts, and radius

00:04:08.808 --> 00:04:10.988
is zero point two one one six nanometers.

00:04:12.428 --> 00:04:16.168
Compare helium plus, which also has one electron but Z equal to two.

00:04:16.948 --> 00:04:20.148
Its ground state energy is minus fifty four point four electron volts.

00:04:20.788 --> 00:04:23.548
The stronger nuclear attraction makes it more tightly bound.

00:04:24.608 --> 00:04:27.768
Do not apply these simple formulas to neutral helium with two electrons.

00:04:28.808 --> 00:04:32.728
Also remember that Bohr orbits are a historical model, not the full modern

00:04:32.728 --> 00:04:34.388
quantum description of electron position.

00:04:35.248 --> 00:04:38.588
The model's useful predictions here are its energy levels and the resulting

00:04:38.588 --> 00:04:39.408
spectral frequencies.

00:04:39.936 --> 00:04:41.096
Now we have levels.

00:04:41.896 --> 00:04:43.216
How does light enter the story?

00:04:43.936 --> 00:04:47.848
An electron making a transition from a higher energy level to a lower one

00:04:47.848 --> 00:04:48.116
emits

00:04:48.116 --> 00:04:49.716
a photon carrying the difference.

00:04:50.636 --> 00:04:54.096
Initial energy minus final energy is positive for that downward transition.

00:04:54.856 --> 00:04:58.816
Do not subtract the magnitudes blindly; write the signed energies first.

00:04:59.476 --> 00:05:03.744
For hydrogen moving from n equal to three to n equal to two, energy released

00:05:03.744 --> 00:05:03.856
is

00:05:03.856 --> 00:05:07.496
thirteen point six times one fourth minus one ninth.

00:05:07.496 --> 00:05:10.556
That is approximately one point eight eight nine electron volts.

00:05:11.676 --> 00:05:15.836
Using h c approximately twelve hundred forty electron volt nanometers, divide

00:05:15.836 --> 00:05:17.496
twelve hundred forty by that energy.

00:05:18.036 --> 00:05:21.876
The wavelength is about six hundred fifty six nanometers, a visible Balmer

00:05:21.876 --> 00:05:22.216
line.

00:05:22.876 --> 00:05:24.996
Let's compare the transition from two to one.

00:05:25.516 --> 00:05:29.676
Its photon energy is ten point two electron volts, giving roughly one hundred

00:05:29.676 --> 00:05:31.596
twenty two nanometers in the ultraviolet.

00:05:32.156 --> 00:05:34.896
Larger energy difference means shorter photon wavelength.

00:05:35.696 --> 00:05:39.756
Pause for a prediction: does an electron at n equal to two need thirteen point

00:05:39.756 --> 00:05:42.356
six electron volts to be removed completely?

00:05:43.156 --> 00:05:43.496
No.

00:05:43.996 --> 00:05:47.836
Its energy is minus three point four, so reaching zero requires three point

00:05:47.836 --> 00:05:49.036
four electron volts.

00:05:49.036 --> 00:05:53.536
Thirteen point six is the ground state ionization energy for hydrogen in this

00:05:53.536 --> 00:05:53.756
model.

00:05:54.416 --> 00:05:58.176
For absorption between discrete bound states, the photon must match an allowed

00:05:58.176 --> 00:06:00.576
energy gap, subject to transition rules.

00:06:01.336 --> 00:06:05.556
Ionization is different: a photon above the ionization threshold can leave the

00:06:05.556 --> 00:06:07.656
released electron with surplus kinetic energy.

00:06:08.516 --> 00:06:10.636
Identify the starting state before choosing a threshold.

00:06:10.896 --> 00:06:14.856
Let's organize the spectrum instead of memorizing isolated colors.

00:06:15.516 --> 00:06:19.756
A spectral series groups transitions that finish at the same lower level.

00:06:20.636 --> 00:06:24.236
Lyman lines finish at one and lie in the ultraviolet for hydrogen.

00:06:25.216 --> 00:06:26.616
Balmer lines finish at two.

00:06:27.396 --> 00:06:30.656
Paschen lines finish at three and lie in the infrared for hydrogen.

00:06:31.316 --> 00:06:37.016
The Rydberg relation gives inverse wavelength as R H times Z squared times the

00:06:37.016 --> 00:06:40.136
difference between inverse squared lower and higher quantum numbers.

00:06:40.696 --> 00:06:43.736
Use the lower index first so the bracket is positive.

00:06:44.476 --> 00:06:48.236
In a given emission series, increasing the starting level makes the photon

00:06:48.236 --> 00:06:50.056
energy approach a limiting value.

00:06:50.736 --> 00:06:54.236
The shortest wavelength is the series limit, not the first line.

00:06:54.856 --> 00:06:59.296
For hydrogen's Balmer limit, the photon energy approaches three point four

00:06:59.296 --> 00:07:03.896
electron volts, giving roughly three hundred sixty five nanometers with our

00:07:03.896 --> 00:07:04.396
rounded constants.

00:07:05.096 --> 00:07:10.038
Now a different kind of JEE question: an ensemble of excited hydrogen atoms

00:07:10.038 --> 00:07:10.236
can

00:07:10.236 --> 00:07:12.736
make transitions among levels one through four.

00:07:13.456 --> 00:07:15.656
How many distinct energy differences are possible?

00:07:16.516 --> 00:07:16.916
Count pairs.

00:07:17.476 --> 00:07:20.116
Four times three divided by two gives six.

00:07:20.736 --> 00:07:24.976
That does not mean one electron emits all six photons during a single downward

00:07:24.976 --> 00:07:25.436
cascade.

00:07:25.956 --> 00:07:30.536
An individual cascade follows one route and can contain at most three downward

00:07:30.536 --> 00:07:32.216
steps from four to one.

00:07:32.936 --> 00:07:36.559
Across many atoms and different routes, the six transition energies can

00:07:36.559 --> 00:07:36.916
appear.

00:07:37.136 --> 00:07:41.576
More detailed quantum transition selection rules are outside this simple

00:07:41.576 --> 00:07:42.476
counting exercise.

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:47.056
Finish by checking the lower series level, the energy sign, and whether a

00:07:47.056 --> 00:07:49.656
question refers to one cascade or an ensemble.
